n. The unit was 18 months past its recommended replacement date.\n\n## Impact\n\n- **Duration**: 2 hours 13 minutes of service disruption\n- **Passengers evacuated**: 7,300 (3,200 from Station 35 + 4,100 from Station 36)\n- **Trains affected**: 12 trains rerouted or held\n- **Estimated economic impact**: ₪450,000\n\n## Key Finding: Shared System Cascade\n\nThis incident demonstrates the cascade effect of shared infrastructure:\n1. Single failure point (Ventilation System A power supply)\n2. Affected TWO stations (Station 35 AND Station 36)\n3. Combined evacuation of 7,300+ passengers\n4. Lesson: Shared systems require redundant power supplies\n\n## Action Items\n\n1. [Climatec Systems] Replace all power supply units on ventilation systems\n2. [NTA] Add redundant power supply to Ventilation System A\n3. [NTA] Review all shared systems for single points of failure\n4. [Shapir Engineering] Update emergency procedures for multi-station events\n5. [Training] Conduct joint evacuation drill for Stations 35 and 36\n\n## Responsible Parties\n\n- **Station 35**: Shapir Engineering (Yossi Cohen)\n- **Station 36**: Shapir Engineering (Yossi Cohen)  \n- **Ventilation System A**: Climatec Systems (maintenance contractor)\n- **Overall Response**: NTA Control Center\n", "\n# Ventilation System A - Technical Specification\n\n## Overview\n\nVentilation System A is a critical shared infrastructure serving both Station 35 \nand Station 36 on the Israel M1 Metro Line. The system provides fresh air circulation, \nsmoke extraction during emergencies, and temperature control.\n\n## System Components\n\n- **Main Fan Units**: 4 x 500 kW axial fans\n- **Backup Fans**: 2 x 250 kW redundant fans\n- **Air Shafts**: 3 vertical shafts connecting to surface\n- **Control System**: Automated with manual override capability\n\n## Stations Served\n\nVentilation System A serves the following stations:\n- **Station 35 (Kiryat Arie)**: Primary ventilation\n- **Station 36 (Shderot HaTzionut)**: Primary ventilation\n\n## Dependencies\n\nVentilation System A depends on:\n- **Central Power Grid**: Primary power source\n- **Emergency Generators**: Backup power (30-minute capacity)\n- **SCADA Control System**: Remote monitoring and control\n- **Fire Detection System**: Triggers smoke extraction mode\n\n## Failure Modes and Impact\n\n### Single Fan Failure\n- Impact: Reduced capacity, system operates at 75%\n- Stations affected: Both Station 35 and Station 36\n- Action: Reduced train frequency until repair\n\n### Complete System Failure\n- Impact: CRITICAL - Both stations lose ventilation\n- Stations affected: Station 35 AND Station 36\n- Action: Immediate evacuation of both stations required\n- Estimated passengers affected: 80,000+ daily\n- Backup: Emergency generators provide 30 minutes for evacuation\n\n## Maintenance Schedule\n\n- **Weekly**: Filter inspection and cleaning\n- **Monthly**: Fan motor inspection\n- **Quarterly**: Full system test including emergency mode\n- **Annually**: Complete overhaul by Climatec Systems Ltd.\n\n## Emergency Contacts\n\n- **Climatec Systems**: 24/7 emergency line\n- **NTA Control Center**: Central monitoring\n- **Fire Department**: Automatic notification on smoke detection\n", "\n# Station 36 (Shderot HaTzionut) Technical Specification\n\n## Overview\n\nStation 36 (שדרות הציונות) is a deep underground metro station located at the \nintersection of Jabotinsky Street and Shderot HaTzionut in Petah Tikva. It serves \nthe Nachalat Yehuda neighborhood and surrounding residential areas.\n\n## Station Specifications\n\n- **Depth**: 25 meters below ground level\n- **Platform Length**: 150 meters\n- **Platform Width**: 12 meters  \n- **Daily Passenger Capacity**: 45,000 passengers\n- **Peak Hour Capacity**: 8,500 passengers\n- **Entrances**: 3 (North, South, and Central Plaza)\n\n## Connected Systems\n\nStation 36 depends on the following infrastructure:\n- **Ventilation System A**: Shared with Station 35 (critical system)\n- **Central Power Grid**: Connected via Transformer 36A\n- **Fire Suppression System**: Connected to Station 35's system\n- **Signaling System**: Controlled by Central Traffic Control\n\n## Adjacent Stations\n\n- **North**: Station 35 (Kiryat Arie) - 1.2 km tunnel distance\n- **South**: Station 37 (Central Hub) - 0.8 km tunnel distance\n\n## Failure Modes\n\nIf Station 36 becomes unavailable:\n1. Passengers must use Station 35 or Station 37 as alternatives\n2. If Ventilation System A fails, Station 35 is also affected (shared system)\n3. Train service may continue through the station without stopping\n4. Emergency evacuation routes lead to both Station 35 and surface exits\n\n## Construction Details\n\n- **Contractor**: Shapir Engineering\n- **Construction Period**: 2019-2024\n- **Method**: Cut-and-cover with TBM tunnel connections\n- **Project Manager**: Yossi Cohen (Shapir Engineering)\n", "\n# Station 35 (Kiryat Arie) Technical Specification\n\n## Overview\n\nStation 35 is an underground metro station serving the Kiryat Arie business district \nin Petah Tikva. It is the northernmost station on the initial M1 segment.\n\n## Station Specifications\n\n- **Depth**: 22 meters below ground level\n- **Platform Length**: 150 meters\n- **Platform Width**: 10 meters\n- **Daily Passenger Capacity**: 35,000 passengers\n- **Peak Hour Capacity**: 6,500 passengers\n- **Entrances**: 2 (Business District and Residential)\n\n## Connected Systems\n\nStation 35 depends on the following infrastructure:\n- **Ventilation System A**: SHARED with Station 36 (critical dependency!)\n- **Central Power Grid**: Connected via Transformer 35A\n- **Fire Suppression System**: Connected to Station 36's system\n- **Signaling System**: Controlled by Central Traffic Control\n\n## Adjacent Stations\n\n- **South**: Station 36 (Shderot HaTzionut) - 1.2 km tunnel distance\n\n## Shared Infrastructure Warning\n\n⚠️ **CRITICAL**: Station 35 shares Ventilation System A with Station 36.\nIf Ventilation System A fails:\n- Both Station 35 and Station 36 lose ventilation\n- Both stations may require evacuation\n- Passengers from both stations affected: ~80,000 daily\n\n## Construction Details\n\n- **Contractor**: Shapir Engineering (same contractor as Station 36)\n- **Construction Period**: 2019-2024\n- **Method**: Cut-and-cover \n- **Project Manager**: Yossi Cohen (Shapir Engineering)\n", "\n# Israel M1 Metro Line - System Overview\n\nThe M1 Metro Line is Israel's first underground metro system, serving the Tel Aviv \nmetropolitan area. The line runs from Petah Tikva in the east to Tel Aviv's central \nbusiness district, with planned extensions to Bat Yam in the south.\n\n## Stations on the Northern Segment\n\nThe northern segment includes the following stations:\n\n1. **Station 35 (Kiryat Arie)**: Underground station serving the Kiryat Arie business \n   district. Connects to Station 36 to the south. Shares Ventilation System A with \n   Station 36 due to their proximity.\n\n2. **Station 36 (Shderot HaTzionut / שדרות הציונות)**: Deep underground station at \n   the intersection of Jabotinsky and HaTzionut. This station connects to Station 35 \n   to the north and Station 37 to the south.\n\n3. **Station 37 (Central Hub)**: Major interchange station connecting M1 to future \n   M2 and M3 lines. All northern stations feed passenger traffic into this hub.\n\n## System Dependencies\n\nCritical infrastructure relationships:\n- Stations 35 and 36 share Ventilation System A (if it fails, both stations affected)\n- All stations connect to Central Power Grid via the main transformer\n- The Signaling System controls train movements between all stations\n- Emergency systems are interconnected for coordinated evacuations\n\n## Construction Contractors\n\n- Shapir Engineering: Responsible for Station 35, Station 36, and tunneling\n- Solel Boneh: Responsible for Station 37 (Central Hub)\n- NTA Metropolitan Mass Transit: Overall project management\n"], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-large"}}, "key": "embeddings_2c9e73b3acb9cdb353a9a679d4f7475afec4be50d708421a6fc1df58235deab0_v2"}